@meta
id: 13-11
title: With or without
chapter: 13
type: lesson
skills: regex-quantifiers
par: 45
minutes: 6
vscode: two separate find-and-replaces, and check neither touched customerEmail
open: notification.provider.ts

@brief
The `customer` object on the notification args has been renamed
`contact`. Four places in this provider read its `email` — two as
`customer.email` and two, where the object might be missing, as
`customer?.email`. Rename the object in all four.

Nothing else with `customer` in it changes: not `customerId`, not
`customerEmail`, not `customer_email` in the template map. The local
`customer` in `fallbackEmail`, at the bottom, is a different object
loaded from the repository and keeps its name — it is the fifth match
of your pattern, and you stop before it.

So the pattern is `customer`, then *maybe* a question mark, then a dot,
then `email`. "Maybe" is a quantifier too: zero or one.

@keys
| `\?` | zero or one of the atom before it — optional |
| `\=` | the same thing, another spelling |
| `?` | on its own, in a forward search, an ordinary question mark |
| `cw` | change the word — stops at the `?` or the `.` on its own |

@hints
One pattern that accepts both spellings, then `cw` at each match and
`.` for the rest.
--
`\?` makes the thing before it optional. A literal `?` needs no escape
in a forward search, so the optional question mark is `?\?` — a
question mark, made optional.
--
`/customer?\?\.email` `Enter`, then `cwcontact`, `Esc`, and `n.` three
times.

@solution
/customer?\?\.email<Enter>  cwcontact<Esc>  n.  n.  n.  :wq<Enter>

Forty keystrokes, one pattern, four edits.

`\?` is "zero or one". `colou\?r` finds both spellings of colour;
`https\?://` finds both kinds of URL; `refunds\?` finds the singular and
the plural. It is the quantifier you reach for when two forms of a thing
are in circulation and you want to treat them as one.

`?\?` looks silly and is worth understanding. The first `?` is a
literal question mark — in a forward search it has no special meaning
at all. The second is the quantifier, applied to it. If that reads
badly to you, `\=` means exactly the same as `\?`, and `?\=` is the
same pattern. The reason vim offers two spellings is that in a
*backward* search, started with `?`, a bare `?` ends the pattern, so
`\?` cannot be typed there and `\=` is the only option.

`cw` did the rename because `customer` is a word and both `?` and `.`
end it. The pattern found four places; `cw` only ever touched the
first word at each. That split — a pattern precise enough to land, an
edit small enough to be safe — is what the whole chapter is building
towards.

@start notification.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { EmailService } from '../../infrastructure/services/email.service';
import { CustomerRepository } from '../customer/customer.repository';

const templates = {
  order_confirmed: 'order-confirmed',
  customer_email_changed: 'email-changed',
  refund_issued: 'refund-issued',
};

@injectable()
export class NotificationProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(EmailService) protected emailService: EmailService,
    @inject(CustomerRepository) protected customerRepository: CustomerRepository,
  ) {}

  public async orderConfirmed(args: { orderId: string; customerId: string; customer?: Contact }) {
    const to = args.customer?.email ?? (await this.fallbackEmail(args.customerId));

    return this.emailService.send({ to, template: templates.order_confirmed, orderId: args.orderId });
  }

  public async refundIssued(args: { refundId: string; customer: Contact }) {
    const customerEmail = args.customer.email;

    return this.emailService.send({ to: customerEmail, template: templates.refund_issued, refundId: args.refundId });
  }

  public async emailChanged(args: { customerId: string; customer?: Contact; previous: string }) {
    const to = args.customer?.email ?? args.previous;

    return this.emailService.send({ to, template: templates.customer_email_changed, previous: args.previous });
  }

  public async digest(args: { customer: Contact; orderIds: string[] }) {
    return this.emailService.send({ to: args.customer.email, template: 'digest', orderIds: args.orderIds });
  }

  public async fallbackEmail(customerId: string) {
    const customer = await this.customerRepository.findOne({ where: { id: customerId } });

    return customer.email;
  }
}

@expected notification.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { EmailService } from '../../infrastructure/services/email.service';
import { CustomerRepository } from '../customer/customer.repository';

const templates = {
  order_confirmed: 'order-confirmed',
  customer_email_changed: 'email-changed',
  refund_issued: 'refund-issued',
};

@injectable()
export class NotificationProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(EmailService) protected emailService: EmailService,
    @inject(CustomerRepository) protected customerRepository: CustomerRepository,
  ) {}

  public async orderConfirmed(args: { orderId: string; customerId: string; customer?: Contact }) {
    const to = args.contact?.email ?? (await this.fallbackEmail(args.customerId));

    return this.emailService.send({ to, template: templates.order_confirmed, orderId: args.orderId });
  }

  public async refundIssued(args: { refundId: string; customer: Contact }) {
    const customerEmail = args.contact.email;

    return this.emailService.send({ to: customerEmail, template: templates.refund_issued, refundId: args.refundId });
  }

  public async emailChanged(args: { customerId: string; customer?: Contact; previous: string }) {
    const to = args.contact?.email ?? args.previous;

    return this.emailService.send({ to, template: templates.customer_email_changed, previous: args.previous });
  }

  public async digest(args: { customer: Contact; orderIds: string[] }) {
    return this.emailService.send({ to: args.contact.email, template: 'digest', orderIds: args.orderIds });
  }

  public async fallbackEmail(customerId: string) {
    const customer = await this.customerRepository.findOne({ where: { id: customerId } });

    return customer.email;
  }
}
